  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial\n\nII. ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $8$\n\nIII. ${x}^{4}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}+2.$ Then $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+2},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{4}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{4}={x}^{4+4}={x}^{8},$ meaning that ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=-{x}^{4}.$ Then ${x}^{4}+P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{4}=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true.",
